{"source_id":"W2052218930","title":"The Pen Is Mightier Than the Keyboard: Advantages of Longhand Over Laptop Note Taking","year":2014,"authors":["Pam A. Mueller","Daniel M. Oppenheimer"],"abstract":"Taking notes on laptops rather than in longhand is increasingly common. Many researchers have suggested that laptop note taking is less effective than longhand for learning. In three experiments, students took notes either longhand or on a laptop while watching a lecture, then completed tests of factual recall, conceptual understanding, and application. In Experiment 1, longhand note takers performed better than laptop note takers on conceptual questions, even though laptop note takers recorded more content verbatim. In Experiment 2, this pattern persisted when participants were instructed to take verbatim notes. In Experiment 3, participants who took longhand notes and then studied their notes performed better on conceptual questions than those who took laptop notes and studied their notes. The authors argue that longhand note taking encourages deeper processing because it requires summarizing and paraphrasing rather than transcription."}
